  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Isolated thunderstorms across southwest Montana and Lemhi County\n  this evening. More widespread showers and thunderstorms\n  Saturday.\n\n- Temperatures cooling through the weekend, running 10 degrees\n  below normal by Sunday\n\nThe Northern Rockies remains sandwiched between a broad low\npressure system over British Columbia and high pressure centered\nover the four corners region. The end result is southwest flow\naloft over western Montana and north central Idaho. Another pulse\nof energy is expected to move through the flow this afternoon and\nevening producing isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms\nacross southwest Montana and Lemhi County. The main storm threats\nwill be gusty outflow winds to 40 mph and lightning. The rest of\nthe area will largely remain dry through this evening, though\nbreezy southwest to west winds will develop. \n\nBeginning tonight into Saturday, the low pressure system over \nCanada will stretch southwards and close off over Washington. \nShowers and isolated thunderstorms will develop late tonight, and \nbecome more widespread through the day Saturday. By Saturday \nafternoon, a cold front will move through the region, which could \nhelp strengthen a few storms, mainly along and south of Highway \n12. Chances for wetting rains (more than 0.10\") reaches 80 to 90 \npercent for west-central and southwest Montana, as well as Lemhi \nCounty. Confidence for wetting rain decreases across northwest \nMontana, where probabilities for wetting rains drop below 30 \npercent.\n\nBy Sunday, temperatures will drop into the upper 60s to low 70 for\nmany valley locations, which is roughly 10 degrees below normal\nfor this time of year. Breezy conditions will continue, thought\nhigher humidities and cooler temperatures will keep the fire\nweather threat low. \n\nBrief high pressure rebounds Monday and Tuesday, which will allow\ntemperatures to warm once again into the 80s. This looks short\nlived however as numerous forecast models are showing the\npotential for a colder and potentially wetter low pressure system\nmid to late next week.",
